  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA much drier airmass will move in behind the cold front tonight\nwith low temperatures falling into the lower to middle 40s \nacross some interior valleys with 50s elsewhere. High pressure \nwill build across New England this weekend, allowing for \ntranquil weather conditions with warm afternoons and cool \nnights. Highs on Saturday will be into the upper 70s to near 80 \nsouth of the mountain with highs on Sunday around 5 degrees \nwarmer. Lows each night will be into the 40s and 50s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper trough swinging through the Canadian Maritimes will send a \ncold front through northern New England Sunday night. This front \nwill bring chances for showers and thunderstorms, mainly from the \nmountains to central Maine and points northward late Sunday. Latest \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance favor mostly dry conditions \nMonday as high pressure builds in from the north and the cold\nfront settles over southern New England as a stationary front.\n\nA compact upper low over Canadian Prairies Tuesday will slowly\ntranslate east through the second half of next week. This\nfeature will attempt to shift the stationary front northward as\na warm front late Tuesday. Models diverge on how far north rain\nchances get on Tuesday with some solutions keeping all but far\nsouthern NH mostly dry. The latest NBM expands chance PoPs\n(30-50%) northeast across the area through Tuesday afternoon\nwhich seems reasonable given the spread in solutions. Model\nspread also exists with respect to the eastward progression of\nthe upper low and a potential leading short wave crossing\nWednesday and Thursday. This has resulted in a broad brush of 50\nto 60 percent PoPs Wednesday through Thursday while it is likely\nthat it will not be raining continuously through this time.\nOverall, the pattern supports some beneficial rainfall from the\nmiddle to end of the work week.",
